{"abstract":"Sphagnum peat bogs store a large fraction of biologically-bound carbon, due to a steady accumulation of plant material over millennia. The resistance of Sphagnum biomass to decay is poorly understood and of high importance for preservation efforts and climate models. Sphagnum peat mostly consists of the polysaccharide-rich cell wall of the moss but the mechanisms by which it resist degradation by microbes remain unclear. Here we show that enzymatic saccharification of peat polysaccharides including cellulose and other glucose-rich polysaccharides is predominantly limited by access to the substrate. The experimental approach involved biotechnological tools including hydrothermal pretreatment to disrupt and relocate cell wall components. This physical change was confirmed by confocal laser scanning microscopy. A cocktail of microbial enzymes (Cellic® CTec3) designed for industrial saccharification of lignocellulose of vascular plants was used to assess enzymatic digestibility of peat polysaccharides. The glucose yield increased from close to zero for untreated peat to 30% and 50% when pretreated at 160 and 180 °C. An overall catalytic rate constant for enzymatic glucose-release from peat-cellulose of 26.98 h-1 was calculated using a kinetic model. This is a similar or higher rate compared to cellulose from vascular plant tissues. With an iron content of 2 g/kg dry peat, oxidative inactivation of enzymes is an important factor to take into account. A high inactivation constant of 125.91 x10-3 h−1 was found for the used saccharification conditions, but the addition of catalase alleviated the oxidative inactivation and increased the glucose yield with 60% in peat pretreated at 180 °C. These findings show that molecular structures of Sphagnum peat which prevents access for cell wall degrading enzymes can be disrupted by hydrothermal pretreatment. This brings us closer to understanding peat recalcitrance and thus how very large amounts of organic carbon is stored.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Thomsen, Jonas","Lett, Signe","Martens, Helle Juel","Sørensen, Helle","Kelleher, Darragh","Tryfona, Theodora","Dupree, Paul","Johansen, Katja S."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-819050"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Cambridge"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"enzymatic saccharification of peat polysaccharides is limited by accessibility","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0312219","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-819050","identifier":[{"id":"105006662792","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC12101845","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"40408618","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-4d0a53c7-c940-4e37-950a-03bef2f47304"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-578D68F3CF2EF74486E1E0421351637E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0312219","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/453620682/Enzymatic_saccharification.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Geosciences and Natural Resource Management, Forest and Bioressources"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of cambridge","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Cambridge","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Jonas","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"1bcdb620-aa6d-484b-adb9-79e6c5552edb","name":"Thomsen, Jonas","orcid":"0000-0001-7534-202X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Signe","key":"lettpau","last":"Lett","locid":"8bd25ea3-200a-4c93-a10b-ce60197f4ed0","name":"Lett, Signe","orcid":"0000-0003-2515-8413","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Helle J.","key":"martenspau","last":"Martens","locid":"cd7f808e-0b4a-4423-9846-d3a81d0fad26","name":"Martens, Helle Juel","name_org":"Martens, Helle J.","orcid":"0000-0002-3011-4557","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Helle","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"426794af-4797-4454-b12a-b63959d15fe2","name":"Sørensen, Helle","orcid":"0000-0001-5273-6093","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Darragh","key":"kelleherpau","last":"Kelleher","name":"Kelleher, Darragh","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Theodora","key":"tryfonapau","last":"Tryfona","name":"Tryfona, Theodora","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Paul","key":"dupreepau","last":"Dupree","name":"Dupree, Paul","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Katja S.","key":"johansenpau","last":"Johansen","name":"Johansen, Katja S.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40408618"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0312219","issn":["19326203"],"issue":"5","journal":"PLOS ONE","paperid":"e0312219","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE","title_org":"PLoS ONE","volume":"20"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105006662792"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-4d0a53c7-c940-4e37-950a-03bef2f47304"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Enzymatic saccharification of peat polysaccharides is limited by accessibility","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-819050","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Basidiomycete fungi are the main decomposers of dead wood with an impact on the global carbon cycle. Their degradative mechanisms have been well-studied under aerobic conditions. Here, we study their activity in oxygen-depleted environments. We use metaproteomics in a field study to identify active wood-decomposing fungi and their enzymes at different depths from the wood surface, including in oxygen-depleted conditions. In vitro, we observe that the brown-rot fungus Fomitopsis pinicola can grow on wood in complete anoxia. Using 13C solid-state NMR, we demonstrate the degradation of plant cell-wall polysaccharides and fungal growth in the absence of oxygen. Proteomic analyses reveal that F. pinicola switches from a Fenton chemistry-based process under aerobic conditions to the secretion of plant cell wall-active enzymes in anoxia. Our finding that wood decay fungi can thrive in complete anoxia provides a deeper understanding of lignocellulose degradation mechanisms in nature and raises opportunities for the development of bio-inspired anaerobic processes.","author_count":23,"contributors":["Röllig, Robert","Lebreton, Annie","Grenga, Lucia","Cresswell, Rosalie","Lett, Signe","Tryfona, Theodora","Navarro, David","Lambert, Julien","Grisel, Sacha","Gimbert, Isabelle","Martens, Helle Jakobe","Miotello, Guylaine","Yu, Xiaolan","Drula, Elodie","Rosso, Marie-Noelle","Tarrago, Lionel","Henrissat, Bernard","Johansen, Katja","Dupree, Ray","Armengaud, Jean","Dupree, Paul","Berrin, Jean-Guy"],"cuid":["S02-098.76-822851"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Berrin, Jean-Guy"],"country":["France","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","INRAE","Paris-Saclay University","University of Cambridge","University of Warwick","Université Paris-Saclay"],"keyword":["Database","Decomposition","GROWTH","Genome","Mechanisms","Oxalate","Secretome analysis","Transcriptome"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"wood decay under anoxia by the brown rot fungus i fomitopsis pinicola i","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-62567-3","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-098.76-822851","identifier":[{"id":"40783394","type":"pmid"},{"id":"105012941884","type":"scopus"},{"id":"001547173700014","type":"wos"},{"id":"PMC12335584","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["dtu-acf4d7a0-3a79-4000-9cee-4348017d3273","ku-243af2e4-79d9-4f74-8539-4fd5fba6dda7"],"keyword":["Database","Decomposition","Genome","Growth","Mechanisms","Oxalate","Postia-placenta","Secretome analysis","Transcriptome"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F564A3C58E3AB48B2A3968758FF022C1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/462205393/s41467-025-62567-3.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/410824748/s41467-025-62567-3.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-62567-3","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"fr","key":"inrae","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"INRAE","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fr","key":"université paris saclay","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Université Paris-Saclay","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"fr","key":"paris saclay university","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Paris-Saclay University","used":1},{