{"abstract":"P5A ATPases are expressed in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) of all eukaryotic cells, and their disruption results in severe ER stress. However, the function of these ubiquitous membrane proteins, which belong to the P-type ATPase superfamily, is unknown. We purified a functional tagged version of the Saccharomyces cerevisiae P5A ATPase Spf1p and observed that the ATP hydrolytic activity of the protein is stimulated by phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate (PI4P). Furthermore, SPF1 exhibited negative genetic interactions with SAC1, encoding a PI4P phosphatase, and with OSH1 to OSH6, encoding Osh proteins, which, when energized by a PI4P gradient, drive export of sterols and lipids from the ER. Deletion of SPF1 resulted in increased sensitivity to inhibitors of sterol production, a marked change in the ergosterol/lanosterol ratio, accumulation of sterols in the plasma membrane, and cytosolic accumulation of lipid bodies. We propose that Spf1p maintains cellular sterol homeostasis by influencing the PI4P-induced and Osh-mediated export of sterols from the ER.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Sørensen, Danny Mollerup","Holen, Henrik Waldal","Pedersen, Jesper Torbøl","Martens, Helle Juel","Silvestro, Daniele","Stanchev, Lyubomir Dimitrov","Costa, Sara","Günther-Pomorski, Thomas","Lopez Marques, Rosa Laura","Palmgren, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-542336"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the p000000000005a atpase spf000000000001p is stimulated by phosphatidylinositol 000000000004 phosphate and influences cellular sterol homeostasis","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1091/mbc.E18-06-0365","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-542336","identifier":[{"id":"30785834","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC6724510","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85064814513","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-f979280c-542b-480a-9320-45e4daca0621"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7BE80D3BF54175005BC91555D9A93071"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1091/mbc.E18-06-0365","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/224336807/mbc.e18_06_0365.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Plant and Environmental Sciences, Section for Transport Biology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Danny Mollerup","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"9184be40-9774-4344-9b35-5612ea22bc29","name":"Sørensen, Danny Mollerup","orcid":"0000-0002-5254-6780","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik Waldal","key":"holenpau","last":"Holen","locid":"a06bb151-1fc7-4a77-9e85-46a05613acfd","name":"Holen, Henrik Waldal","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jesper Torbøl","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"5e499858-1bda-4fae-b0b9-d47fe5e30d8e","name":"Pedersen, Jesper Torbøl","orcid":"0000-0003-3094-1461","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Helle Juel","key":"martenspau","last":"Martens","locid":"cd7f808e-0b4a-4423-9846-d3a81d0fad26","name":"Martens, Helle Juel","orcid":"0000-0002-3011-4557","rank":4,"