"00320889","15322548"],"issn_org":["00320889"],"issue":"4","journal":"Plant Physiology","pages":"1768-1778","publisher":"American Society of Plant Biologists","status":"p","title":"Plant Physiology","volume":"179"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85064216052"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-3165674e-4fcf-4dc2-85b4-bc4100dcf020","ku-e4d6a6fe-6765-4e82-a984-d6be79e3de8a"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Direct Comparison of Leaf Plasmodesma Structure and Function in Relation to Phloem-Loading Type","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-033685","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Shell-loosening is of importance in facilitating shrimp peeling. In this study, enzyme and high pressure (HP) improved the shell-loosening at different degrees, which were observed as gaps by microscopy. The shell-loosening gap induced by an endoprotease with broad specificity (Endocut-03L, 53 μm) was much higher than that induced by HP at 100 MPa (HP100, 12 μm), followed by an endoprotease with high specificity (Tail21, 8 μm), and HP at 600 MPa (HP600, 5 μm). The degree of shell-loosening was found to be correlated to the extent of protein changes that were obtained by 2D gel electrophoresis. Shell-loosening due to HP100 and Endocut-03L was mainly caused by physical and enzymatic degradation of high molecular-weight proteins in shell and epidermis and subsequent loss of degradation products, disrupting the structure of muscle-shell connection. However, HP100 was less effective than Endocut-03L due to its stabilizing effect on the shell collagen, lowering its shell-loosening effect.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Dang, Tem Thi","Jessen, Flemming","Martens, Helle Juel","Gringer, Nina","Olsen, Karsten","Bøknæs, Niels","Orlien, Vibeke"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-035567"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Orlien, Vibeke"],"keyword":["2D gel electrophoresis","Microscopy","Peeling","Protein","Shell loosening","Shrimp"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"proteomic and microscopic approaches in understanding mechanisms of shell loosening of shrimp i pandalus borealis i induced by high pressure and protease","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/251256403/Proteomic_and_microscopic_approaches_in_understanding_mechanisms_of_shellloosening_of_shrimp_Pandalus_borealis_induced_by_high_pressure_and_protease.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The authors are grateful to the Green Development and Demonstration Program (GUDP Denmark), The Danish Agrifish Agency , the Ministry of Food, Agriculture and Fisheries for the financial support to the project “Sustainable technologies for the optimization of shrimp production - TECHSHELL” (Grant number 4009-14-0870 , 2015). Our thanks also go to Tailorzyme ApS (Søborg, Denmark) for kindly donating the enzymes. We thank Hanne Lilian Stampe-Villadsen (National Food Institute, Technical University of Denmark) for her technical assistance with 2D gel electrophoresis, and Catherine Anne Skrzynski Nielsen (Department of Plant and Environmental Sciences, University of Copenhagen) for her technical assistance with microscopy. Imaging data were collected at the Center for Advanced Bioimaging (CAB), University of Copenhagen."],"id":"S02-100.00-035567","identifier":[{"id":"30955673","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85063326081","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-80fe46f8-a025-4da8-9a4f-246291208c76","ku-b027c292-746e-457e-9093-d5f323601653"],"keyword":["2D gel electrophoresis","Microscopy","Peeling","Protein","Shell loosening","Shrimp"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-DF3E46DFF17FAF0C58EACF3511CB23DE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/219905583/Pages_from_FOODCHEM_S_18_06815.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/251256403/Proteomic_and_microscopic_approaches_in_understanding_mechanisms_of_shellloosening_of_shrimp_Pandalus_borealis_induced_by_high_pressure_and_protease.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}]