{"abstract":"The effect of a commercial multienzyme product obtained by fermentation from Aspergillus aculeatus on soybean and soybean meal was investigated using viscosity measurements, dietary fibre component analysis and different microscopy techniques utilizing histochemical dyes and antibody labelling. The results obtained demonstrated a strong viscosity reducing effect of the enzyme preparation on soluble galactomannan and xyloglucan polysaccharides and in addition non-starch polysaccharide analysis demonstrated a notable solubilisation of all polysaccharide constituents. The degradation of these components as native integral parts of cell walls upon exposure to the enzyme was visualized with microscopy. Two histochemical dyes, coriphosphine O and alcian blue were successfully used to follow pectin solubilisation after enzyme treatment. Commercial antibodies recognizing specific components of pectin and hemicellulose components of soybean cell wall were also used to visualize several enzyme activities in the commercial enzyme preparation The challenges of using commercial antibodies elicited from a given plant source to detect similar epitiopes on another plant source are also discussed. Non-starch polysaccharide analysis of the insoluble dietary fibre constituents before and after enzyme treatment corroborated the visualized mode of action demonstrated by microscopy. The combination of techniques provided visual and quantitative measurements of the solubilisation and degradation of hemicellulose pectic soybean cell wall components as part of the undesirable antinutrients in animal feed.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Ravn, Jonas Laukkonen","Martens, Helle Juel","Pettersson, Dan","Pedersen, Ninfa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-450342"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"enzymatic solubilisation and degradation of soybean fibre demonstrated by viscosity fibre analysis and microscopy","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-450342","ids":["ku-5f108e35-845a-4af0-ac51-c76103a430a6"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-9FDF179FBF4CC3F921018CADFF13B52E"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Plant and Environmental Sciences, Section for Transport Biology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Jonas Laukkonen","key":"ravnpau","last":"Ravn","name":"Ravn, Jonas Laukkonen","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Helle Juel","key":"martenspau","last":"Martens","locid":"cd7f808e-0b4a-4423-9846-d3a81d0fad26","name":"Martens, Helle Juel","orcid":"0000-0002-3011-4557","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Dan","key":"petterssonpau","last":"Pettersson","name":"Pettersson, Dan","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Ninfa","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","name":"Pedersen, Ninfa","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5539/jas.v7n9p1","issn":["00218596","14695146"],"issn_org":["19169752"],"issue":"9","journal":"Journal of Agricultural Science","status":"p","title":"Journal of Agricultural Science","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-5f108e35-845a-4af0-ac51-c76103a430a6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Enzymatic solubilisation and degradation of soybean fibre demonstrated by viscosity, fibre analysis and microscopy","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-450342","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"Aims Intercropping legumes and non-legumes may affect the root growth of both components in the mixture, and the non-legume is known to be strongly favored by increasing nitrogen (N) supply. The knowledge of how root systems affect the growth of the individual species is useful for understanding the interactions in intercrops as well as for planning cover cropping strategies. The aim of this work was (i) to determine if different levels of N in the topsoil influence root depth (RD) and intensity of barley and vetch as sole crops or as an intercropped mixture and (ii) to test if the choice of a mixture or the N availability in the topsoil will influence the N uptake by deep roots.Methods In this study, we combined rhizotron studies with root extraction and species identification by microscopy with studies of growth, N uptake and 15N uptake from deeper soil layers, for studying the root interactions of root growth and N foraging for barley (Hordeum vulgare L.) and vetch (Vicia sativa L.), frequently grown in mixtures as cover crops. N was added at 0 (N0), 50 (N1) and 150 (N2) kg N ha−1. The roots discrimination relying on the anatomical and morphological differences observed between dicots and monocots proved to be a reliable method providing valuable data for the analysis.Important Findings The intercrop and the barley attained slightly higher root intensity (RI) and RD than the vetch, with values around 150 crosses m−1 and 1.4 m, respectively, compared to 50 crosses m−1 and 0.9 m for the vetch. At deep soil layers, intercropping showed slightly larger RI values compared to the sole-cropped barley. The barley and the intercropping had larger root length density (RLD) values (200–600 m m−3) than the vetch (25–130) at 0.8–1.2 m depth. The topsoil N supply did not show a clear effect on the RI, RD or RLD; however, increasing topsoil N favored the proliferation of vetch roots in the intercropping at deep soil layers, with the barley:vetch root ratio ranging from 25 at N0 to 5 at N2. The N uptake of the barley was enhanced in the intercropping at the expense of the vetch (from ~100mg plant−1 to 200). The intercropped barley roots took up more labeled nitrogen (0.6mg 15N plant−1) than the sole-cropped barley roots (0.3mg 15N plant−1) from deep layers.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Ramirez-Garcia, Javier","Martens, Helle Juel","Quemada, Miguel","Thorup-Kristensen, Kristian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-446348"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"intercropping effect on root growth and nitrogen uptake at different nitrogen levels","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-446348","identifier":[{"id":"84939557526","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-586d1d3e-2763-4eea-a349-fa502fe6ff96"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6A9A83E6B548A8C548607F49D02E5077"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Plant and Environmental Sciences, Section for Transport Biology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Javier","key":"ramirezgarciapau","last":"Ramirez-Garcia","name":"Ramirez-Garcia, Javier","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Helle Juel","key":"martenspau","last":"Martens","locid":"cd7f808e-0b4a-4423-9846-d3a81d0fad26","name":"Martens, Helle Juel","orcid":"0000-0002-3011-4557","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Miguel","key":"quemadapau","last":"Quemada","name":"Quemada, Miguel","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kristian","key":"thorupkristensenpau","last":"Thorup-Kristensen","locid":"ff5b444c-594e-454d-8cbc-331f49f55066","name":"Thorup-Kristensen, Kristian","orcid":"0000-0001-5476-985X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/jpe/rtu024","issn":["1005264X","17529921"],"issn_org":["17529921"],"issue":"4","journal":"Journal of Plant Ecology","pages":"380-389","status":"p","title":"Journal of Plant Ecology","volume":"8"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84939557526"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-586d1d3e-2763-4eea-a349-fa502fe6ff96"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Intercropping effect on root growth and nitrogen uptake at different nitrogen levels","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-446348","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"The epidermis on leaves protects plants from pathogen invasion and provides a waterproof barrier. It consists of a layer of cells that is surrounded by thick cell walls, which are partially impregnated by highly hydrophobic cuticular components. We show that the Arabidopsis T-DNA insertion mutants of REDUCED WALL ACETYLATION 2 (rwa2), previously identified as having reduced O-acetylation of both pectins and hemicelluloses, exhibit pleiotrophic phenotype on the leaf surface. The cuticle layer appeared diffused and was significantly thicker and underneath cell wall layer was interspersed with electron-dense deposits. A large number of trichomes were collapsed and surface permeability of the leaves was enhanced in rwa2 as compared to the wild type. A massive reprogramming of the transcriptome was observed in rwa2 as compared to the wild type, including a coordinated up-regulation of genes involved in responses to abiotic stress, particularly detoxification of reactive oxygen species and defense against microbial pathogens (e.g., lipid transfer proteins, peroxidases). In accordance, peroxidase activities were found to be elevated in rwa2 as compared to the wild type. These results indicate that cell wall acetylation is essential for maintaining the structural integrity of leaf epidermis, and that reduction of cell wall acetylation leads to global stress responses in Arabidopsis.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Nafisi, Majse","Stranne, Maria","Fimognari, Lorenzo","Atwell, Susanna","Martens, Helle Juel","Pedas, Pai","Hansen, Sara Fasmer","Nawrath, Christiane","Scheller, Henrik V.","Kliebenstein, Daniel James","Sakuragi, Yumiko"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-435118"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"acetylation of cell wall is required for structural integrity of the leaf surface and exerts a global impact on plant stress responses","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2015.00550","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-435118","identifier":[{"id":"PMC4510344","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"26257757","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84937875372","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-45d34d37-3685-4247-9a5e-efb43cb756fc"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0FAD67CDC1454C61152BEEF289B96173"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.frontiersin.org/plant_physiology/10.3389/fpls.2015.00550/abstract","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/153311560/fpls_06_00550.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2015.00550","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Plant and Environmental Sciences, Section for Plant Biochemistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Majse","key":"nafisipau","last":"Nafisi","locid":"28bb0f6e-85ea-49f2-bc5c-d7706c13888c","name":"Nafisi, Majse","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Maria","key":"strannepau","last":"Stranne","locid":"5f1b4b2e-42cd-4b73-96a4-31e3ec9a0f57","name":"Stranne, Maria","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lorenzo","key":"fimognaripau","last":"Fimognari","locid":"ee2dabb4-